TI  - Radiation therapy in functioning and no functioning pituitary neuroendocrine 
      tumor: systematic review of the recent literature after 2011.
PG  - 1468724
LID - 10.3389/fendo.2024.1468724 [doi]
LID - 1468724
AB  - Neuroendocrine pituitary tumor, a benign cells proliferation, can cause 
      significant morbidity due to its local invasiveness and secretory properties. 
      Historically, radiotherapy has been employed as a second or third-line treatment 
      option, with studies dating back to the mid-20th century. However, advancements 
      in radiotherapy techniques, such as intensity-modulated radiation therapy (IMRT), 
      stereotactic radiosurgery, and proton therapy, have revolutionized treatment 
      approaches. This review aims to critically evaluate the recent literature 
      (2011-2022) on the use of radiotherapy in both functioning and nonfunctioning 
      neuroendocrine pituitary tumor. We employed the PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items 
      for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses) methodology to systematically analyze 
      52 articles, focusing on local and hormonal control, radiotherapy protocols, and 
      treatment-related side effects.
