IS  - 0028-3886 (Linking)
VI  - 71
IP  - Supplement
DP  - 2023 Mar-Apr
TI  - The Role of Stereotactic Radiosurgery for Functioning and Nonfunctioning 
      Pituitary Adenomas.
PG  - S133-S139
LID - 10.4103/0028-3886.373631 [doi]
AB  - Pituitary adenomas are benign brain tumors that comprise 10%-20% of all central 
      nervous system neoplasms. In recent years, stereotactic radiosurgery (SRS) has 
      emerged as a highly effective treatment option in the management of functioning 
      and nonfunctioning adenomas. It is associated with tumor control rates frequently 
      ranging from 80% to 90% in published reports. While permanent morbidity is 
      uncommon, potential side effects include endocrine dysfunction, visual field 
      deficits, and cranial nerve neuropathies. In patients where single fraction SRS 
      would pose an unacceptable risk (e.g. large lesion size or close proximity to the 
      optic apparatus), hypofractionated SRS delivered in 1-5 fractions is a potential 
      treatment option; however, available data are limited. A comprehensive literature 
      search of PubMed/MEDLINE, CINAHL, Embase, and the Cochrane Library was conducted 
      to identify articles reporting on the use of SRS in functioning and 
      nonfunctioning pituitary adenomas.
