TI  - Advances in the Imaging of Pituitary Tumors.
PG  - 357-373
LID - S0889-8529(20)30041-4 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.ecl.2020.06.002 [doi]
AB  - In most patients with pituitary adenomas magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is 
      essential to guide effective decision-making. T1- and T2-weighted sequences allow 
      the majority of adenomas to be readily identified. Supplementary MR sequences 
      (e.g. FLAIR; MR angiography) may also help inform surgery. However, in some 
      patients MRI findings are 'negative' or equivocal (e.g. with failure to reliably 
      identify a microadenoma or to distinguish postoperative change from 
      residual/recurrent disease). Molecular imaging [e.g. (11)C-methionine PET/CT 
      coregistered with volumetric MRI (Met-PET/MR(CR))] may allow accurate 
      localisation of the site of de novo or persistent disease to guide definitive 
      treatment (e.g. surgery or radiosurgery).
