DP  - 2017 Nov/Dec
TI  - Amelanotic melanocytoma of the sella mimicking pituitary adenoma.
PG  - 283-287
LID - 10.5414/NP301033 [doi]
AB  - We describe a 50-year-old man with a well-demarcated intra- and extrasellar 
      lesion and clinical signs of a non-functioning pituitary adenoma. 
      Neuropathological examination revealed tumor composed of non-pigmented spindle 
      cells arranged in tightly packed nests separated by delicate vascular septae. 
      There was no significant cellular atypia or mitotic activity, and Ki67-positive 
      nuclei were present in less than 1% of cells. The neoplastic cells were positive 
      for S-100 and vimentin, and negative for numerous cellular markers including 
      HMB45, anti-melanoma cocktail antibodies, and Masson melanin stain. Electron 
      microscopy revealed scattered cells with small numbers of premelanosomes, aiding 
      in the correct diagnosis of an amelanotic melanocytoma. This is the first case 
      report of entirely amelanotic melanocytoma of meninges, and in a very unique 
      location. BRAF was negative supporting the diagnosis of intracranial origin of 
      the tumor. Approximately 2 years after subtotal resection and stereotactic 
      radiosurgery the patient is alive and well with a non-progressive residual 
      tumor. .
