TI  - [Treatment of clinically nonfunctioning pituitary adenomas].
PG  - 71-81
LID - 10.1016/j.endonu.2010.01.005 [doi]
AB  - Clinically nonfunctioning adenomas are the most frequent pituitary macroadenomas 
      in adults. These tumors are characterized by the absence of detectable hormonal 
      hypersecretion and are diagnosed when compression symptoms or hormonal 
      deficiencies occur. The treatment of choice of macroadenomas is surgery, but 
      tumoral resection is often incomplete or the patient develops tumoral recurrence. 
      Medical therapy has been shown to produce modest tumoral reduction in some 
      patients. Postoperative irradiation should be considered in patients with large 
      tumoral remnants or enlargement of remnants during follow-up. Stereotactic 
      radiotherapy has been developed to diminish the long-term complications of 
      radiotherapy. Microadenomas tend to remain small and surveillance alone is 
      recommended. The present article reviews the results of medical, surgical and 
      radiation treatments.
